in @prism

in @mirror/glass/ast/shape
in @mirror/glass/ast/token

# @mirror/glass/ast/shape/out — the `out` shape variant: an export
# boundary declaration.
#
# Per the per-variant file pattern declared in @mirror/glass/ast/
# shape: variants of `shape` may land in their own sub-file when
# they want to declare structure of their own. `out` does — it
# carries the @-ref or short-name it exports.
#
# `type out = reference`
#
# Reads: the `out` shape variant IS a `reference` (the @-path or
# name it exports). The variant carries no other data: the only
# thing an export boundary needs is the ref it exposes. The
# substrate already knows `reference` from @mirror/glass/ast/token
# (`seq(literal("@"), name)`); every property already proven about
# `reference` transfers to `out` by alias.
#
# Legacy correspondence (from @mirror/grammar):
#
#   fixed out_form = seq(literal("out"), whitespace, reference)
#
# The legacy form sequenced the literal keyword + the reference;
# at substrate altitude, the keyword IS the variant name and the
# variant data IS the reference. Same compounding pattern as `in`:
# define the variant once at the substrate; the keyword surface
# falls out.
#
# Note on `out <name>` vs `out @<ref>`:
#
#   - Both surfaces appear in the corpus: `out reflect` (short name)
#     and `out @cogito` (full ref) both land valid at the bootstrap
#     altitude. The substrate-altitude truth is one type: a
#     reference. A short name resolves to the file's own namespace;
#     the @-prefixed form is absolute. The substrate doesn't need
#     to distinguish them at the shape-variant altitude —
#     resolution lives one altitude above (@mirror/glass/ast at
#     the file-level).
#
# Distinction from `in`:
#
#   - `in`  : import boundary — "this glass depends on @<ref>"
#   - `out` : export boundary — "this glass exposes @<ref>"
#
# Same shape; opposite polarity. Both alias `reference` at this
# altitude; the polarity is encoded in the variant name itself.

grammar @mirror/glass/ast/shape/out {
  # The substrate's declaration that the `out` shape variant aliases
  # the `reference` lexical primitive. No record body needed —
  # the variant data IS a `reference`. Every downstream substrate
  # that mentions `out @<ref>` or `out <name>` resolves through
  # this line to a reference at the @mirror/glass/ast/token altitude.
  type out = reference
}

out out
out @mirror/glass/ast/shape/out
